# Core-kernel KCSAN races that do not involve kblocker, one regexp per line.
# syz-manager matches them against the first line of the report
# ("BUG: KCSAN: data-race in ...") and drops matches without saving or
# reproducing them. Keep them exact: "pids_release" alone would also hide
# "pids_release / <kblocker function>". A one-sided race ("race at unknown
# origin") prints a single "func+off/size", a two-sided one "f1 / f2".

# idle loop vs. timer interrupt on the same CPU over the got_idle_tick flag
KCSAN: data-race in (tick_nohz_handler|tick_nohz_idle_got_tick) / (tick_nohz_handler|tick_nohz_idle_got_tick)
# PROVE_RCU check of PF_EXITING while the exiting task sets PF_NOFREEZE
KCSAN: data-race in pids_release\+
# WARN_ON_ONCE(mm == current->active_mm) while lazy-TLB shootdown switches it
KCSAN: data-race in __mmdrop\+
# lockless thread_group_empty() in exit_signals (fast path; the slow path
# rechecks under siglock) and taskstats_exit (accounting only) while a
# sibling thread unhashes itself
KCSAN: data-race in (exit_signals|taskstats_exit|release_task) / (exit_signals|taskstats_exit|release_task)
# hrtimer sleepers (futex wait, nanosleep, poll/select timeouts) read t->task
# while the expiring hrtimer clears it; a stale read only makes schedule()
# return at once, the task is already woken
KCSAN: data-race in (futex_do_wait|do_nanosleep|schedule_hrtimeout_range_clock) / hrtimer_wakeup
KCSAN: data-race in hrtimer_wakeup / (futex_do_wait|do_nanosleep|schedule_hrtimeout_range_clock)
# blk-mq tag allocation: sbitmap scans a word locklessly as a hint and claims
# the bit with an atomic test_and_set_bit, retrying if it lost
KCSAN: data-race in (_find_next_zero_bit|sbitmap_find_bit) / (_find_next_zero_bit|sbitmap_find_bit)
# e1000 reads an RX descriptor's status byte that the (emulated) NIC writes by
# DMA; KCSAN cannot see device writes, so the origin is unknown
KCSAN: data-race in e1000_clean_rx_irq\+
# process_one_work samples the worker's se.sum_exec_runtime (CPU-intensive
# detection) while the scheduler tick updates it. Pinned to the offset of
# that read in this kernel build: other one-sided races in process_one_work
# can mean a misused work_struct, including kblocker's
KCSAN: data-race in process_one_work\+0x284/
# a waking workqueue worker samples its own se.sum_exec_runtime while the
# scheduler tick updates it (called only from the scheduler)
KCSAN: data-race in wq_worker_running\+
# nohz idle balancing scans the idle-CPU mask locklessly; a stale bit only
# skips or wastes one balance pass. kblocker uses no bitmap search helpers
KCSAN: data-race in _find_first_bit\+
